1
00:00:00,395 --> 00:00:03,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Ajahn, I am perplexed.

2
00:00:03,417 --> 00:00:14,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Despite my understanding of the theory and being one of consistent practice, as I believe, I seem to lack intellectual prowess.

3
00:00:16,286 --> 00:00:22,590
[SPEAKER_01]: I believe that acquiring wisdom would make me quick-witted and enable me to teach others eloquently.

4
00:00:24,071 --> 00:00:27,153
[SPEAKER_01]: What could be the reason for not attaining such an ability?

5
00:00:28,809 --> 00:00:30,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, have you attained the ability of wisdom first?

6
00:00:31,354 --> 00:00:33,097
[SPEAKER_01]: I've got all the theory down.

7
00:00:33,538 --> 00:00:34,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's the problem.

8
00:00:36,544 --> 00:00:43,027
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to be quick-witted and be able to convey your wisdom to others, you need to have the wisdom.

9
00:00:44,368 --> 00:00:46,089
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to become an expert for yourself.

10
00:00:46,169 --> 00:00:57,974
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you need to fully understand the way out of suffering and the Buddha's teaching, and then you won't have to plan and think how you can convey that to others, because you will become well-versed in it.

11
00:00:59,677 --> 00:01:05,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the simile in the suttas, when he asked the Buddha, do you have to prepare the answers beforehand when people come to you?

12
00:01:06,404 --> 00:01:08,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, do you have the answers prepared waiting?

13
00:01:08,346 --> 00:01:08,947
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, no.

14
00:01:10,696 --> 00:01:13,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me ask you in return, and then you can understand how it is.

15
00:01:13,277 --> 00:01:16,238
[SPEAKER_00]: And he says, you are a chariot maker master.

16
00:01:16,278 --> 00:01:21,140
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, yes, there is nobody that can match my knowledge of chariots, and how to make them, and how to disassemble them.

17
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:22,460
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows everything inside out.

18
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:26,462
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, well, if somebody were to come and ask you about the chariots, would you need to prepare an answer for it?

19
00:01:27,182 --> 00:01:31,363
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, no, if somebody were to wake me up in the middle of the night, I will give them the answer.

20
00:01:31,383 --> 00:01:35,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's how fully understood I am.

21
00:01:36,265 --> 00:01:38,126
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, he knows all of it.

22
00:01:39,126 --> 00:01:39,906
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the point.

23
00:01:40,967 --> 00:01:44,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you have your abstract theoretical knowledge.

24
00:01:44,268 --> 00:01:45,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you have to start applying it.

25
00:01:47,010 --> 00:01:53,113
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know you're applying it when you're becoming free from suffering on account of your understanding, not on account of your management.

26
00:01:53,633 --> 00:01:58,195
[SPEAKER_00]: When suffering can't touch you to begin with, because your mind doesn't move, because the mind understood the nature of things.

27
00:01:59,936 --> 00:02:03,638
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're developing in that regard, you will be able to convey that to others.

28
00:02:04,218 --> 00:02:07,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, of course, there is the aspect, and that's also mentioned in the suttas.

29
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:10,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Some people are simply more skilled in teaching.

30
00:02:10,582 --> 00:02:12,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Some people are less skilled in teaching.

31
00:02:12,883 --> 00:02:17,926
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's something you can understand only once you have a thing to teach.

32
00:02:19,486 --> 00:02:23,509
[SPEAKER_00]: So now you can think, oh, I must be the type that's not skilled in teaching.

33
00:02:23,589 --> 00:02:25,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you you might not be that type.

34
00:02:25,470 --> 00:02:27,571
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just the fact that now you have nothing to teach, really.

35
00:02:28,588 --> 00:02:37,934
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you develop wisdom correctly, in other words, if you free yourself from suffering correctly, then you'll see how prolific, how skilled in teaching you are.

36
00:02:37,954 --> 00:02:45,919
[SPEAKER_00]: But what I'm saying is that even the least skilled sotāpanna is still far more skilled than anything a puṭṭhujana can even imagine.

37
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:53,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is no way that you would not be able to convey what you fully understood.

38
00:02:53,985 --> 00:02:55,966
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just simply not how things work.

39
00:02:57,137 --> 00:02:59,198
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you can't convey it, it's you haven't understood it.

40
00:03:00,199 --> 00:03:05,262
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what Yanivirova said in his letters, like, don't think you understood something unless you can write it down.

41
00:03:05,882 --> 00:03:10,725
[SPEAKER_00]: And as we talked the other day, we can actually add another one and upgrade that statement.

42
00:03:11,145 --> 00:03:14,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't think you understood something until you can explain it to somebody else.

43
00:03:15,928 --> 00:03:19,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, you can sit down and write it for three or four hours and boil it down.

44
00:03:20,685 --> 00:03:27,468
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you actually try and tell it to somebody who doesn't have three hours to listen to you, can you convey your message?

45
00:03:27,828 --> 00:03:28,908
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you make the point?

46
00:03:30,429 --> 00:03:35,531
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you can't, means there isn't... the width of your understanding is not sufficient.

47
00:03:37,532 --> 00:03:39,633
[SPEAKER_00]: So, trying to explain things even to yourself,

48
00:03:40,555 --> 00:03:41,936
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a useful practice.

49
00:03:42,617 --> 00:03:57,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you're just operating on the mental level of your thoughts and theory, with like half expressed phrases and terms and concepts, yeah, that's just like... you can do that with yourself, because you kind of already know what you're thinking.

50
00:04:00,030 --> 00:04:03,453
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you were to then try and say that, it just comes out completely different.

51
00:04:04,914 --> 00:04:06,976
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it's like, oh, that's not what I wanted to say.

52
00:04:07,016 --> 00:04:08,497
[SPEAKER_00]: I was like, well, that's exactly the problem.

53
00:04:09,990 --> 00:04:24,315
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to have the understanding that would pertain to all these layers, whether it's the most abstract, your own kind of idiosyncratic way of revolving around your thoughts and images, down to saying it to somebody else, telling it to them.

54
00:04:25,935 --> 00:04:30,357
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is the measure of understanding, not the idiosyncratic images that you have and that make you feel good.

55
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:34,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people think I made everything, I made all of the sense in my head.

56
00:04:34,601 --> 00:04:35,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, that's my understanding.

57
00:04:36,562 --> 00:04:44,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's certainly helpful, and that's the beginning, and you should develop it, but you develop it by actually being able to utter it and convey it.

58
00:04:45,586 --> 00:04:48,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Write it down, clarify it, boil it down to a point.

59
00:04:49,508 --> 00:04:57,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if understanding is insufficient, not wide enough, not deep enough, you will not be able to boil it down in any intelligible sense.

60
00:05:01,690 --> 00:05:06,131
[SPEAKER_01]: And not just be repeating what others have said.

61
00:05:06,191 --> 00:05:10,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I can, you know, I can memorize a whole essay.

62
00:05:10,212 --> 00:05:10,852
[SPEAKER_00]: I can memorize.

63
00:05:10,992 --> 00:05:12,613
[SPEAKER_00]: I can be the master of comparisons.

64
00:05:12,893 --> 00:05:13,953
[SPEAKER_00]: I can read all the suttas.

65
00:05:13,973 --> 00:05:16,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Got all my PhDs in comparative studies.

66
00:05:16,794 --> 00:05:19,494
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you say this, I say, oh, yeah, that's another sutta that says that.

67
00:05:19,534 --> 00:05:20,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's irrelevant.

68
00:05:21,255 --> 00:05:22,975
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't care what other suttas say about that.

69
00:05:23,976 --> 00:05:26,018
[SPEAKER_00]: I care about the meaning of this which I just said.

70
00:05:26,538 --> 00:05:32,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you boil down the meaning to me without resorting to any other sutra, any other thing that somebody else said, including the Buddha himself?

71
00:05:33,043 --> 00:05:43,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you, in your own words, boil down the root of suffering that you understood for yourself, the freedom from suffering, the craving, any aspect of the dhamma?

72
00:05:43,411 --> 00:05:43,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Describe it.

73
00:05:46,954 --> 00:05:49,736
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when you realize how inadequate your own

74
00:05:50,806 --> 00:05:53,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Our own idiosyncratic images and thinking is.

75
00:05:53,748 --> 00:05:58,071
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't actually come out of that level and recognize the... No, no, no.

76
00:05:58,231 --> 00:06:00,412
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to be able to convey this.

77
00:06:00,452 --> 00:06:03,694
[SPEAKER_00]: That will be the measure of the strength of my understanding.

78
00:06:04,115 --> 00:06:19,765
[SPEAKER_00]: You will remain in that level and perpetually continue conflating pleasing theoretical fits and abstractions and how it all blends and connections with wisdom and understanding.

79
00:06:21,445 --> 00:06:22,505
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not what it is.

80
00:06:26,486 --> 00:06:36,169
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it kind of reminds me of like a danger of eh... like monastic life in a sense of hermits, let's say.

81
00:06:36,289 --> 00:06:38,570
[SPEAKER_01]: People who go away and just live by themselves.

82
00:06:40,170 --> 00:06:40,350
[SPEAKER_01]: It's...

83
00:06:40,391 --> 00:06:44,092
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like one of the dangers, because then you get locked up in that, what you just said, in your own abstractions.

84
00:06:45,092 --> 00:06:48,073
[SPEAKER_01]: And you never actually realize that you can't actually convey this.

85
00:06:48,233 --> 00:06:48,673
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even...

86
00:06:50,592 --> 00:06:52,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think that's also one of the reasons.

87
00:06:52,674 --> 00:07:01,442
[SPEAKER_00]: It kind of translates to that whole requirement of needing to have at least the right view before you should go in like heightened seclusion that the Buddha described.

88
00:07:01,963 --> 00:07:07,028
[SPEAKER_00]: To be able to contain your mind and also to be able to know where the emphasis is and should be.

89
00:07:07,068 --> 00:07:12,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, the benefit of being questioned and

90
00:07:13,568 --> 00:07:17,652
[SPEAKER_00]: That is... that cannot be stated hard enough, really.

91
00:07:18,192 --> 00:07:25,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that you're pressed for answers, that you're pressed for clarification, that you have people that are able to do that.

92
00:07:27,280 --> 00:07:31,283
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already sending you in the right direction, even if you don't necessarily see it for yourself.

93
00:07:33,525 --> 00:07:35,026
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's often you see in the suttas.

94
00:07:35,066 --> 00:07:41,088
[SPEAKER_00]: They would cross-examine each other, not like for the sake of some impersonal debate, but genuinely, like, how do you understand this?

95
00:07:41,108 --> 00:07:42,289
[SPEAKER_00]: How about this?

96
00:07:42,309 --> 00:07:42,749
[SPEAKER_00]: How about that?

97
00:07:43,209 --> 00:07:45,270
[SPEAKER_00]: They would then actually amplify their own understanding.

98
00:07:47,568 --> 00:08:01,114
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, those who believe they understood things, but haven't fully, but then ask the simple question, and it's just just fumbling, just fumbling for words, just talking in these abstractions, it's like, you know, by the end of the sentence, it makes no makes no sense anymore.

99
00:08:02,135 --> 00:08:13,780
[SPEAKER_00]: So, with cross-examination and interrogation, they would realize, if obviously they're honest, which presumably they were back in the day, when you read that in the Suttas, they realize, oh, yeah, yeah, I was lacking, I was... and this actually is helpful.

100
00:08:14,341 --> 00:08:16,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I know that I'm not clear, because if I were

101
00:08:17,522 --> 00:08:18,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I would have...

102
00:08:18,362 --> 00:08:19,063
[SPEAKER_00]: I would have said so.

103
00:08:19,943 --> 00:08:21,004
[SPEAKER_00]: I would have made it clear.

104
00:08:21,984 --> 00:08:28,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Certainly, to another person who is familiar with this, that I don't have to teach from scratch or something everything about what the Buddha said beforehand and so on.

105
00:08:29,888 --> 00:08:38,392
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to... you need to know that you actually, if you understood things, you should be able to... to boil them down, condense them and explain them in very simple language.

106
00:08:39,925 --> 00:08:47,692
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't need to resort to a complex language and terms to explain things, you know your understanding is decent and certainly along the right lines.

107
00:08:48,773 --> 00:08:50,494
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always room for improvement, basically.

108
00:08:51,075 --> 00:09:05,347
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you can only explain things by resorting to complex terminology that requires years of study from your audience in order to understand it, it means like, well, that's already quite devoid of practical application.

109
00:09:06,183 --> 00:09:09,966
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, you can use complex terms if you want, if there is a reason for it.

110
00:09:10,567 --> 00:09:13,950
[SPEAKER_00]: But you will also be able to use simple and plain language.

111
00:09:15,031 --> 00:09:19,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Because these are pretty much not complex truths that have many moving parts.

112
00:09:20,736 --> 00:09:24,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-ownership, impermanence, suffering...

113
00:09:25,380 --> 00:09:26,601
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing complex about it.

114
00:09:27,342 --> 00:09:28,683
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is a certain order to it.

115
00:09:30,308 --> 00:09:36,547
[SPEAKER_00]: That has to be done rightly, so to see these things, and that can all be explained in a very straightforward language.

116
00:09:39,626 --> 00:09:52,211
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only reason a person would not understand you then, it's not because of the words you used, or because the complexity of your descriptions, it's because they refuse to accept the truth of non-ownership and anicca and so on.

117
00:09:52,251 --> 00:09:54,632
[SPEAKER_00]: And they would not understand what you're saying because of that.

118
00:09:55,112 --> 00:09:59,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of their mental attitude of looking for faults and just ignoring what you're saying.

119
00:09:59,474 --> 00:10:00,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's too unpleasant to accept.

